WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.849 --> 00:00:08.371
Hello, everyone!

2
00:00:14.575 --> 00:00:21.019
Welcome back to Superstars in History— the talk show featuring famous people from the past.

3
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:30.006
I'm your host, Faye Moss, and today's guest is an American abolitionist whose courage will amaze you!

4
00:00:31.106 --> 00:00:38.902
After escaping slavery herself, she bravely led many other slaves to freedom along the Underground Railroad.

5
00:00:39.842 --> 00:01:00.328
Please join me in welcoming Harriet Thank you, Faye!

6
00:01:01.704 --> 00:01:06.388
I know you were born around 1820, and your parents named you Araminta.

7
00:01:07.308 --> 00:01:12.472
They were slaves on plantations in Maryland—does that mean you were born into slavery?

8
00:01:12.492 --> 00:01:15.695
Yes, Faye, I was born a slave.

9
00:01:16.775 --> 00:01:22.079
Back then the United States had slave states in the South and free states in the North.

10
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.804
As slaves, my parents worked day and night in their masters' houses and fields.

11
00:01:28.924 --> 00:01:33.586
I mostly played outdoors and looked after younger children—until around age six.

12
00:01:34.607 --> 00:01:35.507
What happened then?

13
00:01:35.887 --> 00:01:36.787
Did you go to school?

14
00:01:38.168 --> 00:01:38.968
Oh no!

15
00:01:39.108 --> 00:01:43.830
Slaves rarely went to school—in fact, I never even learned to read or write.

16
00:01:44.591 --> 00:01:47.872
Instead my master began hiring me out to work.

17
00:01:48.693 --> 00:01:52.634
For the next few years, I was a weaver's helper, maid, and caretaker.

18
00:01:53.495 --> 00:01:59.490
One mistress whipped me if her baby cried too much—these whippings left terrible scars.

19
00:02:00.531 --> 00:02:01.592
How awful!

20
00:02:02.112 --> 00:02:05.435
And that wasn't even your worst childhood injury, was it?

21
00:02:06.596 --> 00:02:13.202
No, I was about 13 when I entered a store where a slave was fleeing from his overseer.

22
00:02:14.143 --> 00:02:17.886
The overseer picked up a metal weight and flung it at the slave.

23
00:02:18.387 --> 00:02:22.830
But it hit me instead, gashing my forehead and knocking me unconscious.

24
00:02:23.571 --> 00:02:27.513
For the rest of my life, I suffered from headaches and seizures.

25
00:02:28.454 --> 00:02:33.896
Yet the injury had a bright side—I started having powerful visions.

26
00:02:34.496 --> 00:02:36.537
I believed these were messages from God.

27
00:02:37.577 --> 00:02:40.839
Were any of your visions about escaping from slavery?

28
00:02:41.919 --> 00:02:42.400
Oh yes!

29
00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.520
I dreamed about freedom all the time.

30
00:02:45.461 --> 00:02:49.382
As a child I heard plenty of stories about slaves escaping to the North.

31
00:02:50.263 --> 00:02:57.034
There were rumors about an "underground railroad," a secret network of safe houses where runaway slaves could get

32
00:02:57.114 --> 00:03:02.178
help. During childhood you learned some valuable skills from your father.

33
00:03:02.858 --> 00:03:03.899
What did he teach you?

34
00:03:04.999 --> 00:03:10.022
Well, I often worked outdoors with my daddy, chopping wood and plowing fields.

35
00:03:10.643 --> 00:03:12.164
He taught me lots about nature.

36
00:03:12.824 --> 00:03:18.567
He showed me which plants are edible, and how the North Star always shines from above, pointing the

37
00:03:18.627 --> 00:03:19.348
way north.

38
00:03:20.352 --> 00:03:26.616
He also taught me how to move silently through the woods—a skill that was mighty useful later on.

39
00:03:28.336 --> 00:03:33.499
In 1844 you married John Tubman, who was a free man, right?

40
00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:35.220
That's right.

41
00:03:35.701 --> 00:03:40.483
I married John and changed my first name to Harriet, my mother's name.

42
00:03:41.424 --> 00:03:44.766
By now times were growing hard on my master's plantation.

43
00:03:45.746 --> 00:03:50.189
I knew I'd be sold soon, and I wanted to be free like my husband.

44
00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:54.052
One day a white woman stopped to ask about my scars.

45
00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:59.837
She told me where she lived and promised to help me if I ever needed help.

46
00:04:00.818 --> 00:04:03.600
I think that day came in 1849?

47
00:04:04.801 --> 00:04:10.285
Yes, in 1849 I ran away with two of my brothers.

48
00:04:10.986 --> 00:04:16.130
But soon after we started out, they got cold feet—worrying it was too dangerous.

49
00:04:17.171 --> 00:04:22.106
We turned back, but that December I escaped again, this time on my own.

50
00:04:23.027 --> 00:04:28.630
I found the white woman's house, and she told me about Underground Railroad conductors, folks who'd help me

51
00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:30.311
along the journey northward.

52
00:04:31.732 --> 00:04:36.455
I set out, traveling at night and relying on the North Star to guide me.

53
00:04:37.455 --> 00:04:41.718
When I finally reached the free state of Pennsylvania, I felt different.

54
00:04:42.958 --> 00:04:50.297
There was such a glory over everything; the sun came like gold through the trees like heaven!

55
00:04:51.378 --> 00:04:53.938
I had to look at my hands to see if I was the same person.

56
00:04:55.199 --> 00:05:01.280
You walked nearly a hundred miles by yourself in the cold and dark to be free!

57
00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:03.241
What happened next?

58
00:05:04.481 --> 00:05:11.023
In Philadelphia I worked odd jobs, but I often felt lonely, like a stranger in a strange land.

59
00:05:11.963 --> 00:05:14.984
I had dreams about my family and bringing them up North.

60
00:05:15.888 --> 00:05:22.592
Eventually I became a conductor on the Railroad, and in 1850, I led my sister's family out of

61
00:05:22.652 --> 00:05:30.796
Maryland. The next year I went back, and I kept returning, probably close to 13 times.

62
00:05:32.657 --> 00:05:38.860
On one trip I went to fetch my husband, and can you believe it—that man had married another

63
00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:42.902
woman! He ended up staying in Maryland.

64
00:05:43.403 --> 00:05:48.041
In 1857 on another trip, I conducted my parents up north.

65
00:05:49.201 --> 00:05:53.782
Did you ever encounter slave catchers—people who returned slaves to their owners?

66
00:05:54.782 --> 00:05:57.683
I had some close calls, but I never got caught.

67
00:05:58.764 --> 00:06:04.565
I believed God was watching over me, and I had ways of protecting myself, like traveling at night

68
00:06:04.705 --> 00:06:05.665
and in the wintertime.

69
00:06:07.546 --> 00:06:10.627
Oh, and I carried a revolver, just in case.

70
00:06:11.807 --> 00:06:17.393
Many people have compared you to Moses from the Bible, who led slaves out of Egypt.

71
00:06:18.373 --> 00:06:26.815
Yes, people even called me Moses, and stories about Moses reached the ears of slave catchers, but few

72
00:06:26.855 --> 00:06:29.876
guessed that Moses was female!

73
00:06:32.017 --> 00:06:37.938
You stopped conducting around 1860, but you continued to do some dangerous work.

74
00:06:38.658 --> 00:06:46.353
When the Civil War broke out in 1861, you worked for the North as a nurse—and a spy!

75
00:06:46.993 --> 00:06:52.975
The Civil War was partly a fight about slavery between the Union in the North and the Confederates

76
00:06:53.015 --> 00:06:53.616
in the South.

77
00:06:54.556 --> 00:06:59.498
The North wanted to abolish it, while the South wanted to preserve the right to own slaves.

78
00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:02.219
So of course, I did my part.

79
00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:05.961
When Union soldiers got injured, I nursed them.

80
00:07:07.041 --> 00:07:12.924
And since I knew how to move around undetected, I also spied on Confederate soldiers for the Union.

81
00:07:14.137 --> 00:07:19.521
In 1863 I became the first woman ever to lead an armed attack in the U.S.

82
00:07:20.202 --> 00:07:26.307
Our army of black soldiers freed about 750 slaves from three plantations!

83
00:07:27.308 --> 00:07:28.749
That's unbelievable!

84
00:07:29.569 --> 00:07:34.594
In 1865 the North won the war, and slavery was abolished.

85
00:07:35.474 --> 00:07:36.575
What did you do next?

86
00:07:37.616 --> 00:07:41.920
I got married again and joined the suffrage movement to fight for women's voting rights.

87
00:07:43.188 --> 00:07:49.713
I also opened a retirement home for former slaves that had an infirmary that provided free healthcare.

88
00:07:50.974 --> 00:07:57.418
The next time I need to summon up some courage, Harriet, I'm definitely going to think of you.

89
00:07:58.319 --> 00:07:59.580
You're amazing!

90
00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:02.242
We'll let you return to history now!

91
00:08:03.503 --> 00:08:05.084
Thank you kindly, Faye.

92
00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:17.324
For about ten years, from 1850-1860, Harriet Tubman led more than seventy slaves to freedom.

93
00:08:18.744 --> 00:08:24.847
She risked her life as well as her own freedom, and she never got caught or lost a

94
00:08:24.947 --> 00:08:29.489
passenger. Harriet Tubman died in 1913.

95
00:08:30.469 --> 00:08:34.351
Her bravery inspired many others to fight for civil rights.

96
00:08:35.771 --> 00:08:37.272
Thank you for joining us today.

97
00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:42.224
I'll be back soon to interview another superstar from the past!
